# Capture files are a byte-exact contract, not text.
#
# `docs/capture-format-v1.md` §2 states the container rule without an exception:
#
#     Line terminator | LF (0x0A). Never CRLF, on any platform
#
# The producer honours that — it writes LF on Windows like everywhere else. But Git for
# Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites LF to CRLF on **checkout** for any
# file it decides is text, and a `.jsonl` file has no NUL bytes so the heuristic decides it is.
# The blob in the object store stays LF; the file on a reviewer's disk does not.
#
# The effect was that a fresh clone on Windows failed this project's own conformance check —
# the first command the README asks a reader to run:
#
#     FAIL  line 0  [spec 2]  7180 line(s) end in CRLF; the format is LF-terminated
#
# `-text` disables both directions of that conversion, so a capture arrives byte-for-byte as
# the producer wrote it. This matters beyond tidiness: EXT-17 consumes these files against the
# frozen spec, and §14's determinism guarantees are stated in bytes.
*.n8rocap.jsonl -text

# The verdict sidecars the referee writes are compared byte-for-byte between a live run and a
# replay (BTB-REF-4), so they are covered by the same reasoning.
verdicts-*.jsonl -text
